The board shows only 6 disks placed so far, with no immediate 4-in-a-row possible for either player. Consequently the first task is to identify whether the current player (X) can create one or more forcing threats—i.e., any line of three X disks whose fourth cell is empty.  

After gravity is applied, the seven legal columns produce the following rows for an X disk:  
- col 0 → (5,0) – yields only isolated pairs;  
- col 1 → (5,1) – completes the horizontal triplet (5,1)-(5,2)-(5,3) whose open end is (5,0);  
- col 2 → (4,2) – yields only pairs (no three-disk line);  
- col 3 → (1,3) – yields only pairs (vertical line blocked by O);  
- col 4 → (4,4) – yields only pairs;  
- col 5 → (5,5) – yields only pairs;  
- col 6 → (5,6) – yields only pairs.  

Only column 1 therefore creates a forcing threat. O will be obliged to answer in column 0 on the next turn; any other reply allows X to finish the four-in-a-row immediately. No other legal column produces even a single threat, so column 1 is the unique move that seizes the initiative and forces a defensive reply.  

Final Answer: 1

